AWS Cloud Engineer Roadmap – Day 14 Documentation

Topic: Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) – Hands-on Lab


---

Day 14 Objectives

On Day 14, I learned how to work with Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS). The focus was on creating additional block storage for an Amazon EC2 instance, attaching the volume, formatting it with a Linux file system, mounting it, and configuring it to mount automatically after every reboot.

This lab demonstrated how Cloud Engineers expand storage for servers without replacing the EC2 instance.


---

What is Amazon EBS?

According to AWS Cloud Practitioner Essentials (AWS Skill Builder):

Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) is a durable block-level storage service designed for use with Amazon EC2 instances. Amazon EBS volumes behave like raw, unformatted block devices that you can format with a file system and mount to an operating system.

Amazon EBS is designed for workloads that require frequent updates, low-latency access, and persistent storage.


---

Features of Amazon EBS

Persistent block storage for Amazon EC2.

Data remains available even if the EC2 instance is stopped.

Supports snapshots for backup and disaster recovery.

Provides high durability and availability within an Availability Zone.

Supports multiple volume types such as gp3, gp2, io1, io2, st1, and sc1.

Can increase storage capacity without replacing the EC2 instance.



---

Amazon EBS Volume Types

General Purpose SSD (gp3)

Recommended for most workloads.

Delivers consistent performance.

Suitable for operating systems, application servers, and development environments.


General Purpose SSD (gp2)

Previous generation SSD volume.

Performance depends on volume size.


Provisioned IOPS SSD (io1 / io2)

Designed for mission-critical applications.

Suitable for databases requiring high IOPS.


Throughput Optimized HDD (st1)

Designed for frequently accessed HDD workloads.


Cold HDD (sc1)

Designed for infrequently accessed data.



---

Difference Between Amazon EBS and Amazon S3

Amazon EBS	Amazon S3

Block storage	Object storage
Attached to EC2	Accessed through APIs
Low latency	High scalability
Supports operating systems	Cannot host an operating system
Mounted as a disk	Stores objects in buckets



---

Practical Lab Performed

Step 1 – Created an EBS Volume

Created a new 2 GiB gp3 EBS volume in the same Availability Zone as the EC2 instance.

Reason: An EBS volume can only be attached to an EC2 instance located in the same Availability Zone.


---

Step 2 – Attached the Volume

Attached the newly created EBS volume to the running EC2 instance.

AWS assigned the device name:

/dev/xvdf


---

Step 3 – Verified the New Disk

Command used:

lsblk

Purpose:

Displays all block storage devices attached to the Linux instance.

Observed:

xvdf

representing the newly attached EBS volume.


---

Step 4 – Created a File System

Command:

sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/xvdf

Purpose:

Formats the new EBS volume with the ext4 Linux file system.

Observation:

When attempted again after mounting, Linux displayed:

/dev/xvdf is mounted; will not make a filesystem here

Reason:

Linux prevents formatting a mounted volume to avoid accidental data loss.


---

Step 5 – Created a Mount Point

Command:

sudo mkdir /data

Purpose:

Creates a directory where the EBS volume will be mounted.


---

Step 6 – Mounted the Volume

Command:

sudo mount /dev/xvdf /data

Purpose:

Mounts the EBS volume so the operating system can access it.


---

Step 7 – Verified the Mount

Command:

df -h

Purpose:

Displays mounted file systems and available storage.

Observation:

The EBS volume appeared mounted on:

/data


---

Step 8 – Retrieved the UUID

Command:

sudo blkid

Purpose:

Displays filesystem information, including the UUID.

Example:

UUID="295ac1e5-10a8-4a86-9df6-5104d7b6a3ac"


---

Step 9 – Configured Automatic Mounting

Opened:

sudo nano /etc/fstab

Added:

UUID=295ac1e5-10a8-4a86-9df6-5104d7b6a3ac /data ext4 defaults,nofail 0 2

Purpose:

Ensures the EBS volume mounts automatically after every reboot.


---

Step 10 – Verified the Configuration

Command:

sudo mount -a

Observation:

No error was displayed.

Meaning:

The /etc/fstab configuration is correct.


